   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the private housing finance law, in relation to the definition of eligible applicant for the affordable home ownership development program",
    "basePrintNo": "S8164",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S8164-2009",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §1111, Priv Hous Fin L",
    "lawSection": "Private Housing Finance Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S8164\n\nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the private housing finance law, in relation to the\ndefinition of eligible applicant for the affordable home ownership\ndevelopment program\n\nPURPOSE:\n\nThe bill would permit the New York State Affordable Housing\nCorporation (AHC) to award grants to particular public benefit\ncorporations formed to assist municipalities with their housing,\ncommunity development or renewal needs.\n\nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 of the bill would amend Private Housing Finance Law § 1111\n(2) to authorize AHC to award grants to particular public benefit\ncorporations fanned to assist municipalities with their housing,\ncommunity development or renewal needs.\n\nSection 2 of the bill would provide for an immediate effective date.\n\nEXISTING LAW:\n\nUnder current law, AHC is permitted to award grants to various\n\"Eligible Applicants.\" Currently, public benefit corporations such as\ncommunity development or urban renewal agencies that have been fanned\nspecifically to assist municipalities with their housing, community\ndevelopment or renewal needs are ineligible to apply for or be awarded\nthese grants.  Instead, grants are generally awarded to the\nmunicipality and later transferred to a specific public benefit\ncorporation by the municipality, resulting in administrative\ninefficiencies.\n\nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nThis is a new bill.\n\nSTATEMENT IN SUPPORT:\n\nMunicipalities rely upon public benefit corporations such.as\ncon:munity development agencies or urban renewal agencies to finance\nand administer their houing development programs. The proposed\nlegislation would make such public benefit corporations eligible to\napply for AHC grants to help finance their housing programs. The bill\nwould make it possible for grant funds to go directly to the public\nbenefit corporations which finance and administer municipal housing\nprograms, thereby enhancing the efficiency of the AHC program and\nbetter serving local needs.\n\nFISCAL IMPACT:\n\nThis bill would have no impact on State finances.\n\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE:\n\nThis bill would take effect immediately.\n\n",
